{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And the Canaanite heard.</b> We find in the conquests of Joshua: \"the king of Arad, one\" (Joshua 12:14), and it is further written in the Journeys: \"and he dwelt in the Negev, in the land of Canaan\" (Numbers 33:40). Now Scripture would not call the land of Canaan, absolutely (סתם), from across the Jordan eastward, as it says: \"the land of Canaan according to its borders\" (Numbers 34:2). Moreover, it would have been fitting for Moses to apportion the land of the king of Arad to one of the tribes of Israel, and Scripture relates everywhere that the land of Sihon and Og, the two kings of the Amorites, Moses gave to the two tribes and a half-tribe, while the nine tribes and a half-tribe took their inheritance after they crossed the Jordan into the land of Canaan. One might say that Israel devoted their land to destruction and did not settle it at all, but this is not correct. And so Rashi too interprets \"and I devoted their cities to destruction\" (Numbers 21:2) as meaning they consecrated their spoil to the Most High.\n\nNow what seems correct in my eyes is that this king of Arad dwelt in the Negev, across the Jordan westward, in the land of Canaan, along the Jordan at the boundary of the tribe of Judah, near Hebron, which is in the Negev. He heard from afar that the children of Israel were coming, and he came by way of the Atharim to the plains of Moab to wage war against them there. This is the meaning of <b>and [he] heard</b>, and therefore Scripture states <b>and he dwelt in the Negev, in the land of Canaan</b>, for he came from another land to the place where Israel was. And Israel vowed a vow to HaShem that if He delivered him into their hand, they would devote all that was his to HaShem. And Scripture relates that HaShem heard their prayer — they vowed a vow to HaShem and they fulfilled it, for they killed them at that time in the days of Moses, as He commanded: \"Any devoted thing that a man devotes from among mankind shall not be redeemed; he shall surely be put to death\" (Leviticus 27:29) — and they gave all their spoil to the treasury of the House of HaShem.\n\nScripture completes the account here further by relating that Israel also devoted their cities to destruction after they entered the land of Canaan, after the death of Joshua, in order to fulfill the vow they had vowed, and they called the name of the cities Hormah. This is what is stated in the Book of Judges (1:16): \"And the children of the Kenite, Moses' father-in-law, went up from the city of palm trees with the children of Judah into the wilderness of Judah, which is in the Negev of Arad,\" and there it is written: \"And Judah went with Simeon his brother, and they struck the Canaanite who inhabited Zephath, and devoted it to destruction, and he called the name of the city Hormah\" (Judges 1:17) — there this vow was fulfilled. But Scripture completed the account by mentioning the matter here. This is like the passage it stated concerning the manna: \"And Aaron placed it before the Testimony, to be kept, and the children of Israel ate the manna forty years, until they came to an inhabited land; they ate the manna until they came to the border of the land of Canaan\" (Exodus 16:34–35) — and this was after the death of Moses until the day after Passover (Joshua 5:12). And likewise, \"These are the names of the men who shall apportion the land to you\" etc. (Numbers 34:17) is a prophecy that they would be alive and standing, for it is impossible that HaShem would designate men for them based on uncertainty; rather, it was fitting that He command Joshua at the time of the apportionment.\n\nIt is also correct to say that Israel devoted this king and his people to destruction now, in the days of Moses, by the sword, and they called the name of the place of the battle Hormah. And after they crossed the Jordan, Joshua also put to death the king of Arad who reigned afterward, among the kings of Canaan who reigned in those days, and the children of Judah, when they entered their cities, devoted them as well and called the name of the cities Hormah, for they fulfilled the vow their fathers had vowed: \"and I devoted their cities to destruction.\" Therefore it says here, \"and he called the name of the place Hormah,\" and there it is written, \"and he called the name of the city Hormah\" — the name of every city that had belonged to the king of Arad, for they fulfilled their vow and their spoil was consecrated to the Most High.\n\nNow everything mentioned here happened at one time, except for his mention of \"and their cities,\" which was for the future, when they would enter their cities. Therefore he said, \"and I devoted their cities to destruction,\" and did not say \"them and their cities,\" for he mentioned the vow pertaining to the future, since they would die in battle and perish there. And the language of Scripture supports what we have interpreted, for it should have said, \"and He delivered the Canaanite into their hand, and they devoted them and their cities to destruction, and they called the name of the place Hormah.\" But it omitted the pronominal suffixes to allude that He delivered the Canaanite into the hand of those to whom Israel delivered him — some of them now and some of them in the future — for HaShem heard their prayer and they fulfilled their vow." "And the meaning of <b>for Israel came by way of the Atharim</b> — according to Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 21:1) — is that when the spies came and went up into the Negev and returned, the inhabitants of the land grew alert to them, and this Canaanite who dwelt in the Negev heard about them, and came by the way they had traveled until he reached the camp of Israel. And he interpreted well.\n\nAnd our Sages said (Rosh Hashanah 3a): What report did he hear? — Scripture drew an analogy for them in the passage of the Journeys: \"And the Canaanite king of Arad heard, and he dwelt in the Negev in the land of Canaan, when the children of Israel came\" (Numbers 33:40), where it mentions no battle and nothing at all. Therefore they said that the report was concerning the death of Aaron, which is mentioned there. Scripture indicates that the enemies of Israel heard about the death of the righteous man and were roused by it to wage war against them. And so they said (Numbers Rabbah 19:11) that this Canaanite was Amalek, and he did not conquer their land nor apportion it, but he devoted their cities to destruction." "<b>and he took captives from him.</b> This was only a single maidservant — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 21:1) from the words of our Sages. The Sages were moved on this point by their reasoning that Israel would never be defeated by an enemy at all, except at a time when they had sinned — as in the first war against Amalek, because they said, \"Is HaShem among us or not?\" (Exodus 17:7), and in the second, because of their sin regarding the spies, from which Moses had warned them (above 14:41–45). But in every obligatory war, not a single man was missing all the days of Moses. Therefore they interpreted this verse: \"and he took captives from him\" — the captive that he had in his possession, and she is that maidservant whom Israel had previously captured from them — as in the expression \"the firstborn of the maidservant\" (בכור השבי) (Exodus 12:29), which means the firstborn of the maidservant (שפחה), for it does not say \"and he took captives from among them, men or women and children.\" \n\nAnd according to the plain sense of Scripture, the meaning is that this Canaanite did not kill any person from among them, but he captured a few of them, and when HaShem delivered him into their hand they recovered everything and not a single man was missing. Scripture mentions this to make known that because Israel initially saw that the Canaanite had prevailed, they vowed this vow to devote all his spoil to HaShem, and HaShem hearkened to their voice. \n\nAnd it is possible to say further that HaShem was wrathful with this Canaanite because he came from a distant land to wage war against Israel and did not fear Elohim, and HaShem desired that he become a thing devoted to destruction; therefore He strengthened him at first, so that Israel would vow to devote him to HaShem."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The Hebrew גבוה is rendered throughout as 'the Most High' following Rashi's usage which Ramban cites; it refers to HaShem.", 1 "The Aramaic root אתר in Onkelos means 'place'; דרך האתרים is rendered by Onkelos as referring to the route the spies took."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:07:53.585151222Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}